From 7b0e639b5c6a61d7b259534d61aa3801634c0615 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>


Date: Wed, 18 Mar 2026 17:44:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] BUGFIX crash in
 JOIN::compare_costs_of_subquery_strategies

Consider:

select count(t1.a) over () from t1 left join t2 on ... group by ...;

t2 can be eliminated by left-join elimination (due to grouping, the
cardinality remains the same).

Background: please read the comment in existing code "There is
unfortunately a catch when the item is Item_sum".

Now that you have this background. count() references t1 and t2 (in
its used_tables, though actually there is only t1 in the argument),
and in spite of that, we correctly eliminated t2 (we're smart, aren't
we?), however, after such elimination, count() still references t2 in
its used_tables.

So, further code in the optimizer can be confused. For example this
one from calculate_materialization_costs():

for (uint i = 0; i < n_tables; i++) {
      JOIN_TAB *const tab = join->best_positions[i].table;
            join->map2table[tab->table_ref->tableno()] = tab;  <<<<<<
}
table_map map = 0;
for (Item *item : VisibleFields(*inner_expr_list)) {
  map |= item->used_tables();
}
map &= ~PSEUDO_TABLE_BITS;
Table_map_iterator tm_it(map);
int tableno;
double rows = 1.0;
while ((tableno = tm_it.next_bit()) != Table_map_iterator::BITMAP_END)
  rows *= join->map2table[tableno]->table()->quick_condition_rows; <<<<<<

If you assume our query is actually a subquery, transformed to a
semijoin, then:
- the loop over VisibleFields may meet the count()
- so 'map' will alas contain t2
- but the map2table array, which has meaningful cells only for tables
which are part of the subquery at this moment of optimization, does
not contain t2 (which has been eliminated at resolution stage). We
access a wrong cell of it, and crash.

The problem is this left-over dependency in count().

The fix is in multiple changes.

- When we decide to eliminate t2, we have to tell count() it does not
depend on t2 anymore; so we add a call to update_used_tables(). But
update_used_tables() merely uses all_tables_map(). So we change
all_tables_map(), so that it excludes eliminated tables (we now
maintain a map of eliminated table, to this aim). But we also see that
all_tables_map() turns on the first leaf_table_count bits. Which is
not correct: after elimination, we have one table
less (leaf_table_count is down by 1), but there can be a gap in
maps (maybe the eliminated table was not the one with the maximum
table number). So we make all_tables_map() uses m_hwm_free_tableno
too, which allows to avoid the "gap" problem. While at it, we add an
assertion to consume_tableno() to make sure that we do not re-use maps
in gaps.
- With that, we can call update_used_tables() to adjust dependencies
of count(). At the same time, we collect the adjusted dependencies to
make sure the eliminated tables are really gone (assertion).
- So now the maps in used_tables of count() are correct and the crash
in calculate_materialization_costs() is gone.

There was a final problem. Let's now additionally assume that t1 and
t2 are references to a recursive CTRE (see the query in mtr). This
time, the situation is: we have a recursive CTE, referenced multiple
times by the parent query, and one such reference is eliminated.
Each reference has a Query_expression. When optimizing the parent
query, we come to optimizing children query expressions which have not
yet been optimized: referenced CTEs have already been optimized, but
not the non-referenced one, so we optimize it, but this call it
expected to be for subqueries, not CTEs, as it provides an empty
materialize_destination:

query_expression->optimize(thd,
  /*materialize_destination=*/nullptr,...
in sql_select.cc. Which crashes in the call, because a recursive CTE
must have a non-empty materialize_destination (see
 // Recursive CTEs expect to see the rows in the result table
 immediately
  // after writing them.
   assert(!is_recursive());
   in sql_union.cc).

The last fix for this is to skip optimizing derived tables in this
loop: a referenced derived table has already been optimized (as the
existing comment says: "Derived tables and const subqueries are
already optimized"), so if we see a derived table, it's not referenced
and we do not need to optimize it. This has good side-effects on
existing mtr tests.

Other similar loops are changed accordingly for consistency.
